Semaphores are low-level: one mis-ordered wait/signal by the programmer breaks the whole scheme, and the two wait
 calls are not atomic together.

Possible remedies (deadlock-free)
 Allow at most 4 philosophers to sit at the table (extra semaphore room = 4 ) – at least one can always get two chopsticks.

 Pick up chopsticks only if both are available (inside a critical section).

 Asymmetric solution – odd philosophers pick left first, even ones pick right first (breaks the circular wait).
 Resource ordering – number the chopsticks and always pick the lower-numbered one first.

Monitor-based solution (deadlock-free)
The monitor lets a philosopher pick up chopsticks only if both neighbours are not eating, so he either gets both chopsticks or
none – hold-and-wait is eliminated. Each philosopher has a state: THINKING, HUNGRY or EATING and a condition variable
self[i] .


 monitor DiningPhilosophers {
 enum {THINKING, HUNGRY, EATING} state[5];
 condition self[5];

 void pickup(int i) {
 state[i] = HUNGRY;
 test(i); // try to get both chopsticks
 if (state[i] != EATING) self[i].wait(); // else block
 }
 void putdown(int i) {
 state[i] = THINKING;
 test((i + 4) % 5); // left neighbour may now eat
 test((i + 1) % 5); // right neighbour may now eat
 }
 void test(int i) {
 if (state[(i+4)%5] != EATING && state[i] == HUNGRY &&
 state[(i+1)%5] != EATING) {
 state[i] = EATING;
 self[i].signal(); // wake i if it was waiting
 }
 }
 initialization_code() { for (i = 0; i < 5; i++) state[i] = THINKING; }
 }
 /* Philosopher i: DiningPhilosophers.pickup(i); eat(); DiningPhilosophers.putdown(i); */


Why it works: only one process can be active inside the monitor at a time, so the test-and-set of the state is atomic. A
philosopher eats only when neither neighbour is eating – so no deadlock. On putdown the neighbours are tested, so a waiting
neighbour is woken immediately. Starvation is still theoretically possible (a philosopher can be bypassed repeatedly by two
alternating neighbours) and can be removed by adding an ageing/queue-based rule (e.g. serve hungry philosophers in FIFO
order).


