 Q1 (b) [5 marks]

 Analyse the algorithm for process Pi (test_and_set with a shared lock ) and state whether it satisfies the three requirements
 of the critical-section problem. Justify.


 // Shared boolean variable lock, initialised to false
 boolean test_and_set(boolean *target) {
 boolean rv = *target;
 *target = true;
 return rv;
 }
 do {
 while (test_and_set(&lock)) ; /* do nothing */
 /* critical section */
 lock = false;
 /* remainder section */
 } while (true);


The shared variable lock is initially false. test_and_set is an atomic hardware instruction: it returns the old value of the
target and sets the target to true. A process spins until it gets false, thereby acquiring the lock; it releases by lock = false .

Checking the three requirements

 Requirement Satisfied? Justification

 1. Mutual YES Since test_and_set is atomic, only one process can read lock = false and set it to true; every other
 exclusion process reads true and keeps spinning until the owner executes lock = false.

 2. Progress YES If no process is in its critical section (lock = false), a process that wants to enter will find lock = false
 immediately and enter; processes in their remainder section do not take part in the decision, so the
 selection is never postponed indefinitely.

 3. Bounded NO Nothing orders the competing processes. When Pi leaves and sets lock = false, whichever process
 waiting executes test_and_set first (maybe Pi itself again, immediately) wins. A process Pj may lose the race
 every time and wait forever (starvation). There is no bound on how many times others may enter
 before Pj.



 Conclusion: the algorithm satisfies mutual exclusion and progress but NOT bounded waiting, so it is not a complete solution
 of the critical-section problem. Fix: use a waiting[n] array and a key: on exit, Pi scans j = i+1, i+2, … (circularly) for the first
 waiting process, hands over the critical section directly to it (sets waiting[j] = false) – this guarantees any process waits at
 most n−1 turns. Also it wastes CPU (busy waiting / spinlock).




